    description: "IEEE 1547 interconnection standard explained: ride-through, power quality, and anti-islanding requirements — and what they mean for new solar plant designs.",
    h1: "What Is IEEE 1547 and Why Does It Govern Solar Interconnection?",
    answer: "IEEE 1547 is the standard that defines how distributed energy resources — solar, storage, wind — interconnect with the electric grid, and its 2018 revision reshaped the industry. Before 1547-2018, inverters were required to disconnect at the first sign of grid trouble. The new standard flipped the philosophy: modern smart inverters must ride through disturbances, supporting the grid instead of abandoning it. That single change — from mandatory tripping to mandatory ride-through — reflects how much of the grid now depends on inverter-based resources.\n\nThe standard's key requirements: voltage and frequency ride-through curves (the inverter must stay connected through specified excursions, with defined trip settings outside those bands), power quality limits (harmonic distortion, DC injection, flicker), anti-islanding (the inverter must detect when the grid section is de-energized and stop energizing it — a lineworker safety requirement), and interoperability (standardized communication interfaces so utilities can monitor and, where authorized, control the resource). Category I, II, and III performance levels let utilities specify the ride-through robustness their system needs — a weak rural feeder gets different requirements than a strong urban network.\n\nFor project engineers, 1547 shows up in equipment selection and interconnection compliance. Inverters must be listed to UL 1741 SB (the test procedure for 1547-2018 compliance) — the utility will ask for the listing. The interconnection study verifies the plant's 1547 settings against the utility's requirements; some utilities publish their own 1547 implementation guides with specific trip settings and functions (volt-var, volt-watt, frequency-watt) they require enabled. Our interconnection support includes the 1547 compliance documentation — settings files, listing certificates, and the study exhibits — because the utility's interconnection engineer reviews them before approving energization. IEEE 2800 extends similar concepts to transmission-connected inverter plants, and NERC's reliability standards layer on top for bulk-system resources.",
    directAnswer: "IEEE 1547-2018 is the interconnection standard for distributed energy resources. Its core change: smart inverters must ride through grid disturbances (supporting the grid) rather than tripping offline. It specifies voltage/frequency ride-through, power quality limits, anti-islanding protection, and interoperability. Inverters must be UL 1741 SB listed; utilities verify 1547 settings during interconnection review.",
