    slug: "utility-energy-nec-article-690-solar-requirements",
    title: "What Are NEC Article 690 Requirements for Solar PV Installations?",
    description: "NEC Article 690 for solar PV: conductor sizing, overcurrent protection, disconnects, rapid shutdown, and grounding — the code rules behind every design.",
    h1: "What Are NEC Article 690 Requirements for Solar PV Installations?",
    answer: "NEC Article 690 is the code article dedicated to solar photovoltaic systems, and every PV design — from rooftop to utility-scale — is built on it. (Article 705 covers interconnected power production sources more broadly, including storage.) The article's requirements shape conductor sizing, protection, disconnecting means, and grounding across the entire DC and AC collection system. The 2023 NEC cycle refined several provisions, and the authority having jurisdiction enforces the cycle it has adopted — which varies by state and sometimes by city.\n\nConductor sizing starts with the continuous-current rule: PV source and output circuit conductors are sized at 125% of the short-circuit current, with additional temperature and conduit-fill derating. In practice, this means the wire schedule is one of the first calculations in the design — and one of the most common plan-check comments when done sloppily. Overcurrent protection follows: fuses or breakers at 125% of circuit current, with equipment rated for DC service where it sits on the DC side. Combiner boxes, disconnects, and inverters all carry DC ratings that must match the system's maximum voltage — 1000V and 1500V classes dominate utility-scale design, and every component in the string must be rated for the system's voltage.\n\nDisconnecting means are required at multiple levels: the PV system disconnect (readily accessible, lockable open), inverter input and output disconnects, and AC disconnects. Rapid shutdown (690.12) requires controlled conductors to de-energize to safe levels within seconds of initiation — primarily a rooftop firefighter-safety rule, but it shapes equipment selection even on ground-mount projects. Grounding and bonding per 690.41–690.47 covers equipment grounding, system grounding, and ground-fault protection. Labeling (690.31, 690.53) is its own discipline: directory plaques, voltage markings, and arc-flash labels that the inspector checks. Our drawing packages are built to pass electrical plan check the first time — because the NEC articles the reviewer cites are the ones we designed to.",
    directAnswer: "NEC Article 690 governs solar PV: conductors sized at 125% of short-circuit current with temperature derating, DC-rated overcurrent protection and disconnects, rapid shutdown for firefighter safety, grounding and ground-fault protection, and detailed labeling. Equipment must be rated for the system voltage (1000V/1500V classes). Designs are built to pass electrical plan check the first time.",
